<!-- §01 — The Fixed-k Problem -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Fixed-k Problem</div>
<h2>Every Layer Gets the Same Experts — Regardless of What It Does</h2>
<p>Mixture-of-Experts architectures scale transformer capacity efficiently: instead of one giant MLP, each layer contains many smaller "expert" networks and only activates a subset per token. The standard recipe — inherited from Switch Transformer — picks the top-k scoring experts per token per layer, where k is a hyperparameter fixed before training starts.</p>
<p>The problem is that this choice is arbitrary. Layer 1 of a deep language model encodes simple positional and syntactic patterns. Layer 10 integrates global semantic relationships. Claiming that both layers need exactly the same number of experts is a strong, unjustified assumption — yet that is what every sparse MoE model in production today does.</p>
<p>SoftMoE relaxes this constraint by making the number of active experts differentiable. The model can then learn the optimal expert budget for each layer end-to-end from data. The result is striking: given a global budget of 20 expert-activations across 10 layers, the trained model concentrates roughly 50% — ten activations — in just the final three layers, leaving the first seven to share the other half.</p>

<!-- Allocation contrast canvas -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">per-layer expert allocation — 10-layer model, 32 experts per layer</span>
    <div class="alloc-btns" id="alloc-btns-compare">
      <button class="alloc-btn active" data-mode="sparse">Sparse MoE (fixed k=2)</button>
      <button class="alloc-btn" data-mode="soft">SoftMoE (learned)</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="alloc-compare-canvas" style="width:100%;height:280px;display:block"></canvas>
    <p style="margin:.75rem 0 0;font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono)">
      <span id="alloc-compare-caption">Sparse MoE: every layer activates exactly 2 experts, regardless of layer depth or token complexity.</span>
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The non-uniform allocation is not something a researcher specified — it emerged from gradient descent. Early layers handling syntax settle for a single expert; the final layers reasoning about meaning demand four or five. This finding aligns with decades of interpretability research showing that transformer depth encodes progressively more semantic content.</p>
</section>

<!-- §02 — MoE Routing Basics -->
<section id="s-routing" data-code="routing">
<div class="section-no">02 — MoE Routing Basics</div>
<h2>How Expert Selection Works — and Where Gradients Get Stuck</h2>
<p>In a standard sparse MoE layer, the gating network scores every expert for a given input token, then the top-k operator picks the highest-scoring k. Only those k experts do any computation; the rest are skipped. The token's output is a weighted average of the k active experts.</p>

