<p>The critical issue is the red box: top-k is non-differentiable. Gradients from the loss function can flow back into the gating weights <em>before</em> expert selection (via the pre-softmax scores), but they cannot flow <em>through</em> the selection itself. This means two things are forever locked:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The number of active experts k must be fixed before training — it can never be learned</li>
  <li>The same k must apply to every layer — more-complex layers cannot request extra budget</li>
</ul>
<p>SoftMoE's insight is that if you replace the discrete step with a continuous relaxation, both constraints vanish. The routing becomes end-to-end differentiable, and the budget k per layer becomes a learnable parameter like any other weight.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Standard sparse MoE gate</div>
  G(x) = Topk(softmax(x · <span class="math-var">W_g</span>), k)   <span class="math-comment">← k is fixed, ∂G/∂k = 0</span><br>
  <br>
  y = Σᵢ G(x)ᵢ · Eᵢ(x)   <span class="math-comment">← only k of n experts fire</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- §03 — The LapSum Soft Top-k -->
<section id="s-lapsum" data-code="lapsum">
<div class="section-no">03 — The LapSum Soft Top-k</div>
<h2>Continuous Weights That Still Sum to k</h2>
<p>The LapSum operator (Struski et al., 2025) is a differentiable relaxation of the top-k function. Instead of a binary mask, it produces soft weights in [0, 1] that <em>still sum to k</em> — preserving the sparsity budget while enabling gradient flow.</p>
<p>The key idea: define a threshold x̃ by solving LapSum(x̃) = k, where LapSum is a sum of Laplace CDFs over the expert scores. The routing weights become F_Lap(rᵢ − x̃), each lying between 0 and 1. Crucially, this threshold has a closed-form solution — no sorting, no iterative solver, O(n) complexity. And because x̃ is differentiable with respect to the selection parameter k, gradients can now flow all the way through to k itself.</p>

<!-- Hard vs. Soft canvas -->
<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">hard top-k vs. soft top-k — 8 experts, k = 2</span>
    <div class="mode-btns" id="lapsum-mode-btns">
      <button class="mode-btn active" data-mode="hard">Hard top-k</button>
      <button class="mode-btn" data-mode="soft">Soft top-k</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="lapsum-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;display:block"></canvas>
    <p style="margin:.75rem 0 0;font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);font-family:var(--ff-mono)" id="lapsum-caption">
      Hard: binary mask — exactly 2 experts get weight 1.0, rest get 0. Non-differentiable at the selection boundary.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<p>After computing soft weights, SoftMoE applies a truncation operator T(z) = z · [z &gt; τ] that zeros out any weight below a learned threshold τ. Active experts (above τ) receive full gradient; inactive ones receive zero — recovering sparsity while keeping the routing differentiable with respect to k.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">SoftMoE gate — LapSum relaxation</div>
  x̃ : LapSum(x̃) = Σᵢ <span class="math-var">F_Lap</span>(rᵢ − x̃) = k   <span class="math-comment">← solve for threshold</span><br>
  p̃ᵢ = F_Lap(rᵢ − x̃)   &nbsp; p̃ᵢ ∈ [0,1],  Σp̃ᵢ = k   <span class="math-comment">← soft weights, still sum to k</span><br>
  <br>
  G(x) = T(p̃)   <span class="math-comment">← truncate below threshold τ</span><br>
  ∂k/∂η available   <span class="math-comment">← k is now learnable via reparameterization</span>
</div>

<p>Unlike prior soft-routing methods (e.g. Puigcerver et al.), SoftMoE processes each token independently using only that token's own scores. There is no mixing of information across token positions, which is why it remains fully compatible with autoregressive language modeling — a requirement that many soft-routing approaches break.</p>
</section>

<!-- §04 — Learned Allocation -->
<section id="s-allocation" data-code="allocation">
<div class="section-no">04 — Learned Allocation</div>
<h2>Layers That Need More Experts Get More Experts</h2>
<p>Once the number of active experts k per layer is differentiable, we can optimize it. SoftMoE parameterizes a vector η ∈ ℝᴸ — one scalar per layer — and maps it to per-layer allocations via a softmax reparameterization:</p>
<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">per-layer allocation under global budget K</div>
  π = softmax(η)   <span class="math-comment">← unconstrained, end-to-end trainable</span><br>
  kₗ = πₗ · (K − L) + 1   <span class="math-comment">← ensures kₗ ≥ 1 and Σkₗ = K</span>
</div>
<p>The budget K is the global total of expert activations across all L layers. Setting K = 2L matches the compute of a sparse MoE with k=2. The competition is what matters: if layer 10 claims more experts, layers 1–9 must collectively give them up. Gradient descent resolves this competition by discovering which layers benefit most from additional capacity.</p>

