<p>The redistribution happens fast: most of the expert budget shifts to later layers within the first 25,000 training steps. The final converged pattern is striking — layers 8, 9, and 10 each absorb 3–4× more experts than layers 1 and 2. The top three layers together account for roughly 50% of the total expert budget.</p>
<p>This is not a parameter the authors tuned. It is an emergent discovery by gradient descent, corroborated by interpretability research: early transformer layers encode syntax and shallow phrase structure; later layers encode high-level semantics that benefit from a richer mixture of specialized sub-networks.</p>
</section>

<!-- §05 — Per-Token Compute -->
<section id="s-token" data-code="token">
<div class="section-no">05 — Per-Token Adaptive Compute</div>
<h2>Simple Tokens Use Fewer Experts Than Complex Ones</h2>
<p>Learned per-layer allocation changes how many experts a <em>layer</em> uses on average — but soft routing also adapts compute at the <em>token</em> level. Because the truncation threshold τ applies a fixed cut, the actual number of experts that fire for any given token depends on the distribution of its routing scores.</p>
<p>For simple, predictable tokens — articles like "the", common prepositions like "of" — the gating network learns to concentrate weight on a single dominant expert. The rest fall below τ and are skipped. For semantically rich tokens where many experts plausibly apply — "photosynthesis", "Schrödinger", "amalgamate" — weights spread more evenly and more experts fire.</p>

