<p>During training on C4, the average number of active experts per token per layer <em>increases</em> as training progresses — the model learns to use more of its available capacity as it develops more complex internal representations. This is the opposite of what one might expect from a sparsity-focused system: the soft routing finds richer token-specialist patterns over time.</p>
<p>The theoretical worst case — a token with perfectly uniform routing scores — would activate all 32 experts. SoftMoE prevents this with an expansion factor α: the maximum active experts per layer is capped at ⌈kₗ · α⌉ where α ∈ {2, 4}. In practice, near-uniform routing is rare.</p>
</section>

<!-- §06 — Results -->
<section id="s-results" data-code="results">
<div class="section-no">06 — Results</div>
<h2>Better Performance, Fewer Active Experts</h2>
<p>SoftMoE is evaluated on C4 (206B tokens) and OpenWebText (9B tokens) using a 10-layer decoder-only architecture with 32 experts per block, each expert containing 5M parameters (1.63B total). Downstream evaluation covers PIQA, HellaSwag, and ARC-E in zero-shot.</p>

